## 1. Git Diff

`git diff` is used to see the differences between files.

```bash
git diff
It shows changes made in the working directory that are not yet staged.
2. Git Diff After Staging
After adding a file:
git add index.txt
The file moves to the staging area.
To see staged changes:
git diff --staged
or
git diff --cached
3. Compare Working Directory with Last Commit
git diff HEAD
This shows differences between the current working directory/staging state and the latest commit.
4. Compare a Specific File
git diff index.txt
Used to see changes made to a specific file in the working directory.
5. Compare a File with HEAD
git diff HEAD -- index.txt
Shows the difference between the current version of the file and the latest committed version.
6. Compare Two Commits
git diff commit1 commit2
Used to see the differences between two specific commits.
7. Compare Two Branches
git diff main test
Used to compare the differences between two branches.
8. Git Diff Check
git diff --check
Checks for common whitespace errors in the changes.
Git Change Flow
Working Directory
       ↓
    git diff
       ↓
Staging Area
       ↓
git diff --staged
       ↓
Local Repository
       ↓
Compare with commits / branches
Quick Revision
git diff               → Working directory changes
git diff --staged      → Staged changes
git diff --cached      → Staged changes
git diff HEAD          → Compare with latest commit
git diff HEAD -- file  → Compare specific file with HEAD
git diff commit1 commit2 → Compare two commits
git diff main test     → Compare two branches
git diff --check       → Check whitespace errors
